```c
// row-major: sequential in memory -> strong spatial locality
for (int i = 0; i < N; i++)
  for (int j = 0; j < N; j++)
    sum += a[i][j];

// column-major: strides N elements per step -> weak spatial locality
for (int j = 0; j < N; j++)
  for (int i = 0; i < N; i++)
    sum += a[i][j];
```

Same total accesses, same result — but the second loop strides across a full row for every element, so almost every access lands on a new, uncached cache line. On a large matrix this can run several times slower than the row-major version, purely from cache misses.
